1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,320
Zero point one looks simple.

2
00:00:02,525 --> 00:00:04,875
Inside a computer, it never ends.

3
00:00:05,155 --> 00:00:09,685
In binary, one tenth repeats forever, so
the machine chops it off.

4
00:00:09,898 --> 00:00:14,968
In 1991, a Patriot missile battery counted
time in tenths of a second.

5
00:00:15,401 --> 00:00:19,931
Every tick, its clock drifted about a ten-
millionth of a second.

6
00:00:20,588 --> 00:00:20,978
Tiny.

7
00:00:21,195 --> 00:00:24,785
But the Dhahran battery had run for over a
hundred hours.

8
00:00:25,138 --> 00:00:27,248
Three point six million ticks.

9
00:00:27,656 --> 00:00:30,406
Now the clock was a third of a second off.

10
00:00:30,742 --> 00:00:35,155
A Scud covers over half a kilometre in
that time, so the radar looked in the

11
00:00:35,155 --> 00:00:35,852
wrong place.

12
00:00:36,056 --> 00:00:39,666
The Scud hit a barracks, and 28 American
soldiers were killed.

13
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:42,150
The fix arrived the next day.

14
00:00:42,232 --> 00:00:44,222
A reboot resets that clock.

15
00:00:44,458 --> 00:00:47,808
But nobody had said how long was too long.

16
00:00:48,161 --> 00:00:52,631
A tiny error isn't small when it repeats
millions of times.
